   "identity": "Here's a Python function that merges two sorted lists into one sorted list, using a classic two-pointer approach:\n\n```python\ndef merge_sorted_lists(list1, list2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge two sorted lists into a single sorted list.\n\n    Args:\n        list1 (list): A sorted list (ascending order).\n        list2 (list): Another sorted list (ascending order).\n\n    Returns:\n        list: A new sorted list containing all elements from both input lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    merged = []\n    i, j = 0, 0\n\n    # Traverse both lists, comparing elements and adding the smaller one\n    while i < len(list1) and j < len(list2):\n        if list1[i] <= list2[j]:\n            merged.append(list1[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            merged.append(list2[j])\n            j += 1\n\n    # Append any remaining elements from list1 or list2\n    merged.extend(list1[i:])\n    merged.extend(list2[j:])\n\n    return merged\n```\n\nThis function runs in O(n + m) time, where n and m are the lengths of the input lists, and uses O(n + m) extra space for the result. It handles empty lists gracefully and preserves the sorted order."
